<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-eventually-constant-sequences" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Eventually constant sequences
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> of elements <m> x_{ n } \in X </m> of a set <m> X </m> is called <term> eventually constant </term> if there is a point <m> x \in X </m> and a natural number <m> n_{ 0 } \in \N </m> so that <m> x_{ n } = x </m> for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ 0 } </m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that any eventually constant sequence of points in a metric space is convergent.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be an eventually constant sequence of points <m> x_{ n } \in X </m> in a metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho } </m>. Then there is a point <m> x \in X </m> and a natural number <m> n_{ 0 } \in \N </m> so that <m> x_{ n } = x </m> for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ 0 } </m>. Let <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> be a positive real number, and observe that
        
        <md>
          \rho \of{ x_{ n } , x } = \rho \of{ x , x } = 0 \lt \epsilon
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ 0 } </m>. We conclude that the sequence <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> x </m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that any sequence of points in a discrete metric space is convergent if and only if it is eventually constant.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of points <m> x_{ n } \in X </m> in a discrete metric space <m> \of{ X , \delta_{ X } } </m>. If <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is eventually constant, then it is convergent by (a) above. Thus we may suppose that <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to some point <m> x \in X </m>.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        Let <m> \epsilon = \frac{ 1 }{ 2 } </m>. Since <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m> is positive, there is a natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that
        
        <md>
          \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ n } , x } \lt \epsilon = \frac{ 1 }{ 2 }
        </md>
        
        for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. Since the values of <m> \delta_{ X } </m> are <m> 0 </m> and <m> 1 </m>, we conclude that
        
        <md>
          \delta_{ X } \of{ x_{ n } , x } = 0
        </md>
        
        and hence <m> x_{ n } = x </m> for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>. Thus <m> \of{ x_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> is eventually constant.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Give an example of a sequence of real numbers which converges in the Euclidean metric <m> d </m> on <m> \R </m> but diverges in the discrete metric <m> \delta_{ \R } </m> on <m> \R </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        <m> \of{ \frac{ 1 }{ n } }_{ n = 1 }^{ \infty } </m> converges to <m> 0 </m> in the Euclidean metric, but this sequence is not eventually constant and so does not converge in the discrete metric.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>
